==English==

===Etymology===
{{prefix|en|in|opportune}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[unsuitable|Unsuitable]] for some [[particular]] [[purpose]].
#: {{ux|en|That was a most '''inopportune''' spot for a picnic.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1847|authorlink=Charlotte Brontë|first=Charlotte|last=Brontë|title={{w|Jane Eyre}}|chapter=XVIII|passage="It appears I come at an '''inopportune''' time, madam," said he, "when my friend, Mr. Rochester, is from home; but I arrive from a very long journey, and I think I may presume so far on old and intimate acquaintance as to install myself here till he returns."}}
# Happening/occurring at an [[inconvenient]] or [[inappropriate]] [[time]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''inopportune''' arrival of the bus cut short the interesting conversation.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|author=G. Freeman Allen|title=The New Look in Scotland's Northern Division&mdash;II|journal=Modern Railways|page=170|text=The object is to keep the yard operators apprised of main-line movements, so that they do not plan to occupy the main lines with activity into or out of the yard at an '''inopportune''' juncture.}}

